 **Note:** Run each test command after a change that could affect the result. After a clean run, don't repeat the same command unless the code has changed since — re-running on unchanged code adds no confidence.
+
+## Local adaptation (this box: jj/stg, quick triggers)
+
+This skill also fires on `/tdd`, "follow TDD" and "test first".
+
+Red-seam proof when a fix already exists in the working tree (e.g. asked to
+"follow TDD" mid-task, or local changes for the bug already exist):
+`git stash push -k -- <production files>` to temporarily drop ONLY the
+production fix while keeping the new test, re-run the test, observe
+failure, then `git stash pop` and re-run to observe pass. Under stg the
+equivalent is refreshing/popping the fix patch while the test patch stays
+pushed. Skip only when no fix exists yet, or when the test fails to compile
+against the unfixed tree (the compile error itself is the demonstrated
+failure — say so explicitly).
+
+Additional rules for this box:
+- One bug = one new dedicated test (or small cluster). Never piggy-back
+  the repro inside an existing scenario test.
+- Do not change the public API just to make the test expressible; put new
+  state on the existing data type instead.
+- No production code first unless no test harness exists; then create a
+  minimal repro script or document the exact manual repro before fixing.
+- Keep tests focused on behavior, not implementation details.
+- Run the focused test first, then broader relevant tests if risk warrants.
+  Always report the failure observed before the fix and the pass after.
